 `regress` is the quick end-to-end check that the build still gives the built-in table's scores.  It solves `--from 1
 --to 400` by default, and takes the `--tactic` flags.  A score that differs stops it, as it stops any run.
 
+```
+$ taxman floor 1000 315426 --threads 2 --progress 0   # does any game of 1000 score more than 315426?
+n=1000  score=315426    held: no game scores more, so opt(n) <= 315426  floor=24168 root_bound=24382 nodes=1902 0.139 s  active=0.118 s  cpu=0.211 s
+$ taxman floor 1000 315425 --threads 2 --progress 0
+n=1000  score=315425    beaten: a game scores 315426, which is opt(n)  floor=24167 root_bound=24382 nodes=2054 0.160 s  active=0.137 s  cpu=0.222 s
+  moves: [997, 961, 713, 989, ...]
+```
+
+**`floor N SCORE`** asks whether any game of N scores more than SCORE, by an exact search from the *floor* SCORE less
+`in_the_bag` ([design/search.md](design/search.md#the-starting-incumbent-the-ceiling-and-the-seed), the paragraph "A
+floor"): no start, seed, ceiling or finder, and nodes pruned as against a set worth the floor.  It sets the board up as
+`solve` does, and writes no results line, since a floor that holds is a bound and not a game.
+
+* *held*: the search ended having found nothing above the floor, so opt(N) ≤ SCORE ([the floor lemma
+  (R5)](../../docs/theory/search-rules.md#the-floor-lemma)).  With SCORE the best-known score plus d, it proves that
+  the best-known score is within d of opt(N), by a search whose gap is the root's bound less the floor.
+* *beaten*: the search found a game that scores more, printed with its moves on the next line: opt(N) when the search
+  ended, and a lower bound on it when a limit stopped it.
+* *open*: a limit or a signal stopped the search before it found anything above the floor.
+
+The line gives the floor and `root_bound`, the root's GF(2) bound, as promotion sums, the steps, and the time, active
+time and CPU over every invocation.  It takes `--threads`, `--tactic`, `--background`, `--checkpoint-dir`,
+`--checkpoint-interval`, `--progress`, `--time-limit`, `--node-limit` and `--mem-cap` as `solve` does.  A search that
+stops keeps its checkpoint `nNNNN.ckpt` and its clock in the directory, and the same command resumes it; the
+checkpoint's header records the floor, so `solve`, and a floor of another SCORE, refuse it.  The outcome is checked as
+`solve` checks a game: the moves replay to their score, and where the built-in table has the game, a floor that held is
+at least its score and a game found is no more than it.  A SCORE below `in_the_bag`, which the moves with no promotions
+beat, is refused.
+
 ```
 $ taxman verify-results results/optimal.jsonl.gz  # replay every game of the committed chain; see section 4
 $ taxman tables 85 8 45                         # the maximal factors and composites of 8 and 45 on the board 1..=85
