@@ -327,6 +327,17 @@ soon as its set reaches it. The start is offered to the run's control as it is m
 before the engine starts, and the engine's start is unchanged. `board::Board` is the one place that knows the path from
 the tables to the frames, the prepared search, the ceiling and the seed.
 
+*A floor.*  A search can start instead from a *floor* (`search::search_floor`, which `taxman floor` runs): an incumbent
+worth a given promotion sum, with no set, no start made and no finder.  The search prunes nodes against the floor as
+against a set of that weight, and a set it finds heavier becomes the incumbent as usual.  A search that completes with
+none found has proven that nothing is heavier than the floor ([the floor lemma
+(R5)](../../../docs/theory/search-rules.md#the-floor-lemma)), which `SearchResult::floor_held` says.  Its tree is that of
+a search from an incumbent of that worth: at P\* the proof's tree, node for node, as a search seeded with an optimal set
+makes it; above P\* a smaller one, by a factor of about 27 for each quarter of the gap between the root's bound and P\*,
+on the games measured ([searches under a
+floor](https://github.com/bvchess/taxman-notebook/blob/main/search/proving-an-upper-bound-part-of-the-way-searches-under-a-floor-2026-10/README.md)).
+Its checkpoint's header records the floor, so it resumes only under the same floor ([trust.md](trust.md)).
+
 ## The near-repeat cut
 
 Consecutive games often pose nearly the same problem, and the proven game before closes most of such a search.
